---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'syntax', title: 'Syntax' },
  { id: 'map', title: 'Map<K, V>' },
  { id: 'map-keys', title: 'Which types can be a key' },
  { id: 'set', title: 'Set<T>' },
  { id: 'canonical-order', title: 'Canonical ordering' },
  { id: 'fixed-arrays', title: 'Fixed-size arrays T[N]' },
  { id: 'nesting', title: 'Nesting' },
  { id: 'cycles', title: 'Cycle detection' },
  { id: 'schema-lock', title: 'Schema lock' },
  { id: 'generated', title: 'Generated code' },
];
---

<DocsLayout title="Collections — IonPath" current="/guides/collections/" toc={toc}>
  <div class="animate-fade-in-up">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Collections</h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8">Ion has four collection shapes: the variable-length <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T[]</code> it always had, and three new ones — <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Set&lt;T&gt;</code> and the fixed-size <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T[N]</code>. All three sort or size-check on write, so the same logical value produces the same bytes on every runtime.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-12 animate-fade-in-up-delay-1">

    <section>
      <h2 id="syntax" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Syntax</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4"><code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Set</code> are ordinary builtin generics, declared beside <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Maybe</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Partial</code>. Unlike those three they have <strong>no shorthand suffix</strong> — there is no <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T{}</code> — so they are always written out:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Inventory</span> {'{'}
    counts:  <span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">string</span>, <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>&gt;;
    tags:    <span class="type-name">Set</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">i4</span>&gt;;
    checksum: <span class="type-keyword">u1</span>[<span class="text-accent-2">32</span>];       <span class="text-text-muted">// exactly 32 elements</span>
    history: <span class="type-name">Event</span>[];          <span class="text-text-muted">// any number of elements</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">All four accept the ordinary modifier suffixes, so <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map&lt;string, i4&gt;?</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[16]?</code> are optional collections.</p>
      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 mt-4 border-l-2 border-accent/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">Arity is checked</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary">A generic used with the wrong number of arguments is <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0060" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0060</a>, and that check covers the three older wrappers too. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Maybe&lt;A, B&gt;</code> and a bare <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array</code> used to resolve silently; they no longer do.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="map" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Map&lt;K, V&gt;</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A <strong>definite-length CBOR map</strong> whose entries are written in canonical key order.</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Element</span>
          <span>Encoding</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="text-[12px]">Container</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">CBOR map. Writers emit a <strong>definite</strong> length; readers also accept an indefinite-length map (<code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0xbf … 0xff</code>).</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="text-[12px]">Key</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">The key type's own Ion encoding. Keys are sorted; see <a href="#canonical-order" class="text-accent hover:underline">canonical ordering</a>.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="text-[12px]">Value</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">The value type's own Ion encoding. Values take no part in the ordering.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="text-[12px]">Duplicate key</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary"><strong>Rejected</strong> on read. Last-wins and first-wins both make the decoded value depend on wire order, which is the non-determinism the ordering exists to remove.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">Readers also accept an unsorted payload; re-encoding canonicalises it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="map-keys" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Which types can be a key</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A key must be a type that has a single canonical encoding <em>and</em> value equality in every target language. That is a shorter list than "anything comparable":</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Allowed</span>
          <span>Rejected — <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0061" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0061</a></span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">the integral scalars <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">i1 i2 i4 i8 i16 u1 u2 u4 u8 u16</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bool</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">duration</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">string</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">guid</code>, and any <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">enum</code></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">every float, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">decimal</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bigint</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bytes</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">datetime</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">dateonly</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">timeonly</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">uri</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">void</code>, a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code>, a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">union</code>, a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">flags</code>, any collection, and any <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T?</code> or <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T~</code></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-oled-border-subtle bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2">Why floats are excluded</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">-0.0</code> and
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0.0</code> encode to different bytes but compare equal, and
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">NaN</code> does not compare equal to itself. Either way a decoded map gains or loses
          entries relative to the one that was written — <strong>a float-keyed map cannot reproduce its own key set.</strong> That is a correctness
          failure, not a style preference, which is why the compiler refuses it rather than warning.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">decimal</code> and
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bigint</code> are excluded for the neighbouring reason: they are arbitrary-precision, so
          ordering by bytes is not ordering by value. <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bytes</code> and the date/time types are excluded because at
          least one target maps them to a reference-equality type, so a decoded map would not find its own keys.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">A <a href="/guides/typedefs/" class="text-accent hover:underline">typedef</a> is judged on what it erases to, since the wire only ever sees the underlying type. The diagnostic names both: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">'Price' cannot be a Map key: it is an alias for 'f8', which is a floating point type…</code></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="set" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Set&lt;T&gt;</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4"><strong>CBOR tag 258</strong> — the IANA-registered "set" tag — wrapping a definite-length array whose elements are in canonical order.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-text-muted">// CBOR diagnostic notation for Set&lt;i4&gt; holding 1000, -1 and 2</span>
<span class="text-accent-2">258</span>([<span class="text-accent-3">-1</span>, <span class="text-accent-3">2</span>, <span class="text-accent-3">1000</span>])</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">The element type is not restricted the way a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> key is. Sorting a set only needs a total order on the encoded bytes, which every type has; a map additionally needs the decoded keys to compare equal to each other, which is the part floats and reference types fail.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="canonical-order" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Canonical ordering</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Both <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Set</code> sort by <strong>RFC 8949 §4.2.1 canonical order</strong>: compare two encoded items by their <em>byte length first</em>, and only then lexicographically by their bytes.</p>
      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 mt-4 border-l-2 border-accent/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">Length-first is not plain bytewise comparison</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary">Integer keys make the difference visible. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">-1</code> encodes as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">20</code> (1 byte) and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">1000</code> as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">1903e8</code> (3 bytes). Length-first puts <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">-1</code> first; a bytewise-only sort would put <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">1000</code> first, because <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">0x19 &lt; 0x20</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">The reason for sorting at all is that a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Dictionary</code>, a JavaScript <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> and a Rust <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">HashMap</code> have three different iteration orders, and one of them is seeded per process. Without a total order the same logical map produces different bytes on every run — which breaks hashing, signing and content-addressing.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="fixed-arrays" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Fixed-size arrays <code class="font-mono">T[N]</code></h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A definite-length CBOR array of <strong>exactly</strong> <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">N</code> elements. Any other length is a typed decode error naming both the declared size and the size found — the length check <em>is</em> the feature.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Transform</span> {'{'}
    matrix: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>[<span class="text-accent-2">16</span>];
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3"><code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">N</code> must be at least <strong>1</strong>. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[0]</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[-3]</code> are <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0062" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0062</a>: an array of no elements encodes nothing, so it cannot be told apart from the field not being there. A size of exactly 1 is odd but coherent and is allowed.</p>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-oled-border-subtle bg-oled-elevated p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-2"><code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">u1[32]</code> is <em>not</em> a byte string</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          It would be a tempting optimisation and it is deliberately not done. A fixed array of
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">u1</code> encodes as 32 CBOR integers in a CBOR array, exactly like
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">i4[32]</code> encodes as 32 integers. Special-casing it would make the CBOR <em>major type</em> of an
          array depend on its element type, which no reader could predict from the array shape alone. Use
          <code class="bg-oled-base px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">bytes</code> when you want a byte string.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="nesting" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Nesting</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Generic arguments may themselves be generic, optional, arrays, or inline types. This is new: a nested argument used to be a hard parse error, because an argument could only be a bare identifier — <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array&lt;User&gt;</code> read as the identifier <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array</code> followed by an unexpected <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">&lt;</code>, so the whole declaration died. One level of generics worked and two never did.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Catalogue</span> {'{'}
    byTag:    <span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">string</span>, <span class="type-name">Array</span>&lt;<span class="type-name">Product</span>&gt;&gt;;
    featured: <span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">string</span>, <span class="type-name">Product</span>?&gt;;
    matrices: <span class="type-name">Set</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">u4</span>&gt;[];
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Nesting is bounded at 32 levels, shared with inline-type nesting, so a pathological input fails with an ordinary parse error rather than overflowing the stack.</p>

      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">
        Nesting is carried all the way through — parser, resolver, diagnostics,
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">ion.lock.json</code> and every code generator.
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map&lt;string, Array&lt;Product&gt;&gt;</code> generates
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Dictionary&lt;string, IonArray&lt;Product&gt;&gt;</code> in C# and
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">HashMap&lt;String, Vec&lt;Product&gt;&gt;</code> in Rust.
        Before <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> existed no type had more than one argument, and nested
        arguments had in fact never worked: the grammar stopped at the inner <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">&lt;</code> and the
        resolver discarded nesting and modifiers even when handed them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="cycles" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Cycle detection</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0030" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0030</a> follows only edges that a value <em>must</em> contain. An edge that can terminate on the wire breaks a cycle; one that cannot does not.</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Edge</span>
          <span>Cycle-breaking?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">T? &nbsp; T~ &nbsp; T[] &nbsp; Map&lt;K,T&gt; &nbsp; Set&lt;T&gt; &nbsp; union arm</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary"><strong>Yes.</strong> Each can encode as empty or absent, so the recursion has a base case.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">T &nbsp; T[N] with N ≥ 1</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary"><strong>No — owned.</strong> A fixed array of at least one element always contains an element, so it cannot terminate.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px] mt-4">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Tree</span> {'{'} kids: <span class="type-name">Tree</span>[]; {'}'}        <span class="text-text-muted">// fine — an empty array terminates</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Graph</span> {'{'} peers: <span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">string</span>, <span class="type-name">Graph</span>&gt;; {'}'}   <span class="text-text-muted">// fine — an empty map terminates</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Bad</span> {'{'} kids: <span class="type-name">Bad</span>[<span class="text-accent-2">4</span>]; {'}'}         <span class="text-text-muted">// ION0030 — 4 children, each with 4 children, …</span></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="schema-lock" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Schema lock</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">All four shapes are recorded in <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">ion.lock.json</code> in one uniform <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Name&lt;args&gt;</code> form, so a reader and a differ only ever have to know that one shape:</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Written</span>
          <span>Recorded</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">Map&lt;string, Document&gt;</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Map&lt;string, Document&gt;</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">Set&lt;i4&gt;</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Set&lt;i4&gt;</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">f4[16]</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Array&lt;f4, 16&gt;</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-keyword">f4[]</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Array&lt;f4&gt;</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">The size goes <em>inside</em> the brackets rather than as an <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Array&lt;f4&gt;[16]</code> suffix so that nested positions stay parseable: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[16]?</code> records as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Maybe&lt;Array&lt;f4, 16&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3"><strong>Changing <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">N</code> is a breaking change.</strong> Because the size is part of the recorded type, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[16]</code> → <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">f4[8]</code> is <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0022" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0022</a>, exactly like changing the element type. It has to be: an old writer emits 16 elements and a new reader demands 8, so the decode fails outright.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="generated" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Generated code</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle">
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Ion</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">C#</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">TypeScript</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-text-muted font-medium">Rust</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">Map&lt;K, V&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Map&lt;K, V&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">HashMap&lt;K, V&gt;</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-oled-border-subtle"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">Set&lt;T&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">HashSet&lt;T&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">Set&lt;T&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">HashSet&lt;T&gt;</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono type-keyword">T[N]</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonArray&lt;T&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">IonArray&lt;T&gt;</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs">[T; N]</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">
        Rust is the only target whose type system can carry the size, so it emits a const-generic
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[T; N]</code>. C# and TypeScript keep their ordinary array type and pass
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">N</code> to the runtime at the call site, so the length is checked on both read and
        write — a wrong length is a typed error naming both lengths, never a silent truncation.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">
        The wire format above is pinned by golden vectors in
        <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">/tests/golden/collections.golden.json</code>, which all three runtime test suites read from
        the same file — including a pair proving that two maps built in opposite insertion orders serialise to identical bytes.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">All three code-generation targets support the container types; there is no target that refuses them.</p>
    </section>

